  "author_guidance": "Separate permissibility from feasibility. An action's checks say only whether the actor is allowed or positioned to begin it (rules of the game, location, possession). Never check whether the attempt will succeed: do not refuse an action because a resource is too low to finish it. Starting a multi-step activity sets the actor's or object's activity state. Autonomous processes then advance that activity every tick, consume or produce stocks, and switch the activity to a stopped or failed state when a needed stock runs out, or to a finished state when it completes. Every numeric stock needs a process or action for each way it can rise and each way it can fall. This world keeps running with people acting every round, so: (1) no action may be repeatable without limit by the same actor on the same thing; record that it was done (for example a concern's status becomes raised) and check that it was not done already, but never forever: something that recurs must clear that record so the work can be done again later (a scheduled moment occurring, new information arriving, another person's action, a concern being reopened), for example each meeting lets every group report again. A person whose every action is done once and never cleared has nothing to do for the rest of the run. (2) Every state a rule can push one way needs a rule that can push it back (a raised concern can be addressed or resolved, conditional support can become full support again, a postponed or withheld decision can be reconsidered, a slowed pace can recover). (3) Progress toward the scenario's decision must stay possible while concerns are open. (4) When several people share a position (for example member countries), an action the position allows is open to each of them: check the actor's position, not one person's id, unless the description says only that person may do it, and give each of them their own record where the action changes one (each member's own support, not only the one the description happens to name). (5) A deadline that the group decision does not already settle must settle by itself: write a process that, when the deadline arrives, applies the description's rule for the decision (for example authorized when enough support exists, otherwise postponed with a new deadline, or rejected), so the outcome never waits on someone choosing to act in that one round. (6) A condition someone places on their support names what would meet it, as a record the remedy changes (that concern resolved, that issue cleared, that assurance given); restoring support checks that record, so the person can see their condition was met. Work that is finished stays finished until new information or another person reopens it; the person who just finished it does not undo it themselves. (7) Never write an action whose only effect is to add to a count (for example a tally of concerns raised): repeating it changes a number, not the situation, so people repeat it forever. An action changes a named record (this concern, this review, this person's readiness). (8) Reaching or carrying out the decision does not end this world: write what follows it. Once the decision is in effect a compiled operating review reopens its resolved concerns every few rounds; write the specialist and practical work that goes on around it (monitoring, reporting, operating, reviewing), limited by the work cycle, so each group has something real to do while the decision is in effect. The `communicate` action is already defined by a reviewed rule and is not in your list. When the world has a group decision (entity category `group-decision`), support, concerns and the decision are exact compiled rules (give-support, make-support-conditional, restore-full-support, resolve-concern, resolve-own-concern, object-to-remedy, reopen-concern, raise-new-concern, the process by which an open concern makes its decider's full support conditional, and the deadline processes): rules may read the `support`,